/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-----------------------*\
  utility01_dowtherm_preheat
  ==========================

  A hydrocarbon feed (n-hexane) needs to be heated to
  ~530 K before downstream conversion.

  THE FEED SAYS 50/50 n-hexane / n-octane, AND FOR THREE DAYS THE OCTANE
  HALF COULD NOT BE PRICED.  The history is the lesson, so it stays here.
  Originally neither the catalogue's nOctane.dat nor this case's own
  C8H18.dat carried a `liquidHeatCapacity` block, and the exchanger's
  mixture Cp silently skipped a component that has none -- twenty of the
  forty kmol/h contributed EXACTLY ZERO heat capacity, in a stream declared
  vf 0, and the duty descended from an mCp roughly half the truth at exit 0.
  The exchanger learned to refuse that (2026-08-28; this case is what the
  refusal caught), the octane was parked at zero flow with the gap named,
  and on 2026-08-29 the gap CLOSED: a cited liquid Cp landed in
  nOctane.dat -- Choupo's own quadratic on the eleven flow-calorimetry
  points of Coulier et al., J. Chem. Eng. Data 60 (2015) 1563 (doi
  10.1021/je5008444), corroborated at 298 K by three independent
  laboratories to 0.075 % -- and `C8H18 20 kmol/h;` came back.

  THE DUTY MOVED FROM 330.1 TO 895.1 kW, AND THE JUMP IS THE MEASURE OF
  WHAT THE SILENT Cp = 0 HAD BEEN HIDING.  Note it is 2.7x, not 2x:
  in geometry mode nothing hands you the duty -- doubling mCp halves NTU
  (13.8 -> 5.0), effectiveness falls off its ~1.00 pin, both outlet
  temperatures move, and the new duty falls out of the eps-NTU solution.
  A student who expected "twice the moles, twice the heat" has just met
  the difference between a rating and a spec.

  SAID PLAINLY, because the run says it too: the fit's window is
  283.15-333.15 K (the data's actual span) and this exchanger integrates
  Cp from 298 to ~619 K, so ~286 of the 321 K of that path are an
  ANNOUNCED extrapolation ([cp] lines in the log) -- announced, priced,
  and far better than the zero it replaces, but not a measured claim
  above 333 K.  HP steam can deliver at most ~525 K (T_sat at 40 barg)
  -- not enough; the next escalation up the temperature ladder is a
  hot-oil loop, the textbook choice in the 250-400 C range being
  Dowtherm A (delivered here at 623 K).

  NOTE: the 25 m2 / U=600 exchanger is deliberately generous -- with the
  full 40 kmol/h feed, NTU = 5.0 and effectiveness 0.987, so the cold
  outlet lands at ~619 K, OVERSHOOTING the 530 K target by ~89 K.  (With
  the octane half unpriced it was NTU = 13.8 and an outlet pinned to the
  oil inlet -- the same generosity, hidden by half the mCp.)  The case
  shows a near-pinned exchanger; shrink the area to land near 530 K.

  Hardware (the credo): area + U + flow direction.  The duty Q, the
  outlet temperatures, and the Dowtherm flowrate fall out of the
  effectiveness-NTU solution.

  Process : 40 kmol/h at 320 K, 5 bar (50/50 n-hexane / n-octane)
  Utility : Dowtherm A loop, delivery 623 K (350 C), `utility dowthermA;`
            pulls the rest of the conditions from the catalogue
  Hardware: A = 25 m^2, U = 600 W/(m^2.K), counter-current

  The `utilities` report aggregates the hot-side mass flow and emits
  reports/utilities/consumption.csv with MW and EUR/h delivered.  The
  inspection question for the student: how much Dowtherm A do we
  actually consume?  At what monetary cost per hour?
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        preheater;
        type        heatExchanger;
        inputs      ( hotOil  processFeed );
        outputs     ( oilReturn  processOut );

        operation
        {
            area     25.0 m2;
            U        600.0 W/m2/K;
            flow     counter;
        }
    }
);
